{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>When Leah saw that she had stopped bearing</b> — I do not know what this matter was with Leah, and why she gave her maidservant to her husband when she herself was not barren that she might build up seed from her, and it is not the way of women to multiply wives for their husbands. But one must say that they were prophetesses (Genesis Rabbah 72:6), knowing that Jacob was destined to establish twelve tribes, and she desired that the majority of his sons come from her or from her maidservant who was under her authority, and that her sister would not prevail over her in sons. Therefore she said, \"Elohim has given me my hire, that I gave my maidservant to my husband\" (see Zohar Vayetze 157a), and likewise Jacob heeded her for this reason — that he would establish many sons — for he knew thus, according to the words of our Sages (Genesis Rabbah 68:11). And it is possible that since they knew that the land was given to their seed, and Abraham and Isaac did not have many sons, Jacob desired many wives to multiply his seed to inherit the land, for the fourth generation would return there (Genesis 15:16). Therefore she wanted to give him her maidservant so that he would not marry a foreign woman:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The final word נכרית could mean 'a foreign woman' or 'a stranger'; rendered literally as 'a foreign woman.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:36:45.628651020Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}